WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.031 --> 00:00:02.414
A variable is like a container for storing

2
00:00:02.474 --> 00:00:03.215
data values.

3
00:00:03.856 --> 00:00:05.097
It has a name that you use to

4
00:00:05.178 --> 00:00:07.040
reference the value stored inside.

5
00:00:07.681 --> 00:00:09.383
Variables can hold different types of

6
00:00:09.423 --> 00:00:11.406
data, like numbers or text.

7
00:00:11.966 --> 00:00:13.308
Let's look at that in a picture.

8
00:00:13.829 --> 00:00:15.651
Here, you can see how a variable has

9
00:00:15.691 --> 00:00:16.792
a name and a value.

10
00:00:17.373 --> 00:00:19.216
The name helps us identify what we're

11
00:00:19.236 --> 00:00:19.736
working with.

12
00:00:20.237 --> 00:00:22.179
And the value is the actual data we

13
00:00:22.219 --> 00:00:23.541
want to keep track of.

14
00:00:24.162 --> 00:00:26.586
When naming variables, it's helpful to use

15
00:00:26.647 --> 00:00:28.730
descriptive names that reflect the value

16
00:00:28.770 --> 00:00:29.111
stored.

17
00:00:29.792 --> 00:00:31.355
You can start with a letter or an

18
00:00:31.415 --> 00:00:34.080
underscore, and then use letters, numbers,

19
00:00:34.301 --> 00:00:35.583
or underscores after that.

20
00:00:36.064 --> 00:00:38.348
Just remember, you can't use spaces or

21
00:00:38.368 --> 00:00:40.532
other special characters, except for

22
00:00:40.632 --> 00:00:41.193
underscores.

23
00:00:41.955 --> 00:00:43.938
This way, your variable names will be

24
00:00:44.018 --> 00:00:46.002
clear and easy to understand.

25
00:00:46.690 --> 00:00:48.412
You can create a variable using the

26
00:00:48.472 --> 00:00:50.694
assignment operator, which is the equals

27
00:00:50.794 --> 00:00:52.356
sign '='.

28
00:00:52.356 --> 00:00:54.478
For example, let's say we want to create

29
00:00:54.518 --> 00:00:57.001
a variable called 'my_variable' and store

30
00:00:57.021 --> 00:00:58.022
the value 10 in it.

31
00:00:58.543 --> 00:01:00.885
We write it like this: my_variable equals

32
00:01:00.905 --> 00:01:01.186
10.

33
00:01:01.706 --> 00:01:04.109
Now, let's see how this looks in code.

34
00:01:04.129 --> 00:01:05.791
When we run this code, we can print

35
00:01:05.831 --> 00:01:07.112
the value of 'my_variable'.

36
00:01:07.713 --> 00:01:08.553
It shows us 10.

37
00:01:09.134 --> 00:01:11.837
This is how we store values in variables.

38
00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:14.262
To use the value stored in a variable,

39
00:01:14.563 --> 00:01:16.004
you simply reference its name.

40
00:01:16.665 --> 00:01:19.108
For instance, if we have 'my_variable' set

41
00:01:19.128 --> 00:01:21.270
to 10, we can update it by adding

42
00:01:21.290 --> 00:01:21.951
5 to it.

43
00:01:22.412 --> 00:01:25.055
We write: my_variable equals my_variable

44
00:01:25.115 --> 00:01:25.715
plus 5.

45
00:01:26.196 --> 00:01:28.318
When we print 'my_variable' again, it

46
00:01:28.399 --> 00:01:29.260
shows us 15.

47
00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:31.782
This way, you can see how easy it

48
00:01:31.802 --> 00:01:34.045
is to update values in variables.

49
00:01:34.767 --> 00:01:36.938
Now that you know about variables, next

50
00:01:36.959 --> 00:01:39.110
you'll explore data types in more detail.

51
00:01:39.834 --> 00:01:41.945
After that, we'll learn about lists and

52
00:01:41.985 --> 00:01:43.513
how to store multiple values.

53
00:01:44.217 --> 00:01:46.268
Understanding variables will help you as

54
00:01:46.328 --> 00:01:48.439
you continue your programming journey.

